# ENCY extension — how this repo works

This is an extension for ENCY CAM, published to the ENCY Extension Store by CI.

**The API reference is not in this repo.** Read it before writing extension code — the rules here
describe the repository, not the API:

- [CAM API reference](https://docs.encycam.com/CAMAPI/3/en/) — every interface, property and method.
- [Lessons](https://docs.encycam.com/CAMAPI/3/en/src/Lessons/Main.html) — the same API in order,
  starting from a first extension.
- [cam-api-examples/docs](https://github.com/EncySoftware/cam-api-examples/tree/v3/main/docs) —
  a worked example of every extension kind.

**If you write a PowerShell script for the author, run it as**
`powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -NoProfile -File <script.ps1>`. A client Windows blocks `.ps1` by
default ("running scripts is disabled on this system"), and a first-time author cannot tell that error
from a broken script. Prefer no script at all: publishing here needs none.

## Anatomy (all under `src/`)

- `ExtensionFactory.cs` — the entry point. The class MUST stay `CAMAPI.ExtensionFactory`
  (namespace + name is how ENCY finds it). It maps extension **id strings** to instances.
- `*.settings.json` — declares the extensions inside this dll for ENCY. Each declared `id`
  MUST have a matching branch in `ExtensionFactory.Create`. `module_path` must point at the
  built dll name: `${extensionJsonFolder}/<AssemblyName>.dll`.
- `Extension.cs` (`UtilityExtension`) — the actual logic. A utility implements `IExtension` +
  `IExtensionUtility` and does its work in `Run(IExtensionUtilityContext, out TResultStatus)`.
  Exceptions do not cross the host boundary: catch everything, report via `TResultStatus`.
- `package.info.json` — store metadata. Keep `packageId` = assembly name. `tags` MUST keep
  the `ency-extension` marker or the catalog will not index the package. `sdkVersion` drives
  the "minimal ENCY version" hint — bump it together with the `EncySoftware.CAMAPI.Sdk.Net`
  package version. Do not set `iconPath` (packing chokes on it today).
- `readme.md` — becomes the store card. Write it for users, not developers.

## Rules

- **This template targets ENCY 3.** The SDK is `EncySoftware.CAMAPI.Sdk.Net` **3.0.8** — the 3.x
  line; a newer 3.x on the public feed is fine to move to. Do NOT swap it for a 2.x version: 2.x is
  the previous generation of ENCY, and an extension built against it is an extension for the old
  product.
- Target is `net10.0-windows`, following the application: ENCY 3 runs on .NET 10 and loads the
  extension into its own process. Do not lower it — a net10 build will not load in an older net8
  ENCY, but that is the version being written for.
- The SDK package comes from the public `ency-master` feed (see `nuget.config`) — do not add other
  ENCY feeds.
- Build for packing with `dotnet build src -c Release -t:PackReady` (normal build + sweeps
  the SDK doc-xmls out of the output; the output folder is packed into the package as-is).
- CAMAPI COM objects should be wrapped in `ComWrapper<T>` (see `Extension.cs`) and disposed.
- Version is stamped from the git tag at publish (`v1.2.3` → `1.2.3`); the value in
  `package.info.json` only matters for local packing.

## Which kind of extension to write

Pick the entry point FIRST, then read its rule before writing any code.

| The extension should... | Kind | Rule to read |
|---|---|---|
| add a button to the utilities menu | utility | `type-utility.mdc` |
| run code when ENCY starts or exits | global | `type-global.mdc` |
| change how another utility runs | utility runner | `type-utility-runner.mdc` |
| add a right-click item on an operation | operation popup | `type-operation-popup.mdc` |
| add a right-click item on a geometry node | geometry node popup | `type-geom-node-popup.mdc` |
| compute a toolpath itself | operation solver | `type-operation-solver.mdc` |
| post-process a calculated toolpath | CLData converter | `type-cldata-converter.mdc` |
| talk to a PLM system | PLM | `type-plm.mdc` |

`ency-cookbook.mdc` applies to all of them: COM lifetime, error handling, parameter input, windows.

This template ships a **utility**. Changing the kind means changing three things together: the class
(its role interface), the key in `*.settings.json`, and the branch in `ExtensionFactory.Create`.

## Publishing

**Starting from nothing?** `create_extension_folder(name, dir)` from the same MCP server makes
the project from the template on this machine — no GitHub account, no git; write the code in its
`src/`, then publish it as below.

**Preferred: `publish_folder(name, folder)` from the `ency-extension-store` MCP server** — no git,
no gh. The store creates the repository in the author's GitHub account, commits this folder into
`src/`, runs the build and returns the result: the version and the card link, or the failing step
with its log (fix the code, call it again). The author is needed twice, in the browser only — the
store sign-in and, once, the app's consent page — and the tool opens both itself. Server not connected? Install it once, no questions asked: `dotnet tool install -g EncySoftware.ExtensionStoreMcp`. Run that yourself — it asks nothing — rather than handing it to the author, then ask the author to
restart the editor: this repository carries `.mcp.json` and `.cursor/mcp.json` that register the server
(a folder made before they existed: `ency-extension-mcp setup`, also yours to run). The server is connected but has no `publish_folder`? The tool is old — update it yourself, it asks
nothing: `dotnet tool update -g EncySoftware.ExtensionStoreMcp`, then ask the author to restart the editor. The same route from a terminal: `ency-extension-mcp publish-folder <Name> <folder>`.

**Never run `gh auth login` for the author** — it stops on a Y/n prompt that swallows the next
pasted command. Without the tool, the same route is the store page: https://apps.encycam.com/publish
→ **Code in a folder** → pick this project's folder → **Upload and publish**.

From a terminal where git and gh are already set up, push a version tag — CI builds, packs and
publishes to the store:

```bash
git tag v0.1.0 && git push --tags
```

Or use the `ency-extension-store` MCP tools when available: `publish_extension` (tags and
pushes for you), then `publish_status` (run status → store card link). A NEW extension lands
hidden until a store moderator approves it; the direct card link works immediately. New
versions of an approved extension go live without moderation.
